Hi Pat,

Here is our October update on the calendar sync integration between our room-=
booking system
and your visitor kiosks. It's longer than usual because a lot moved this mont=
h.

Where we are. The one-way sync is finished on our side: when someone books a =
meeting room and
adds an outside guest, the guest's name, host and arrival time now reach your=
 kiosk's expected
visitors list within about a minute. We ran it for two weeks on our own offic=
e's kiosk and
logged 214 bookings. Three arrived late, all on the Monday our network provid=
er had its outage,
and all three caught up once the connection came back.

What changed. Your team's note about time zones was right. Bookings made by s=
taff travelling
abroad were showing the guest's arrival in the booker's time zone, not the bu=
ilding's. We now
send every time in the building's own zone and include the zone name, so your=
 kiosk doesn't have
to guess. The fix went live on 19 October and we haven't seen the problem sin=
ce.

Cancellations. A cancelled booking now removes the guest from your list, as a=
greed. A booking
moved to another day removes the guest and adds them again on the new day, ra=
ther than editing
the old entry. That's simpler for both systems, but it means a host who moves=
 a meeting twice in
an hour will see two removal notices. We think that's acceptable; say if you =
disagree.

What we still need from you. Two small things, neither urgent. First, the tes=
t account you gave
us expires at the end of November; could someone extend it to the end of Febr=
uary? Second, we'd
like the field name your kiosk uses for a guest's company, so we can fill it =
in rather than leave
it blank. Our developer guessed "organisation", but your sandbox rejected it.

Two-way sync. This is the part we've both been waiting for: when a guest sign=
s in at your kiosk,
our system would mark the meeting as started and tell the host. We have a wor=
king draft, but it
needs your sign-in events, which your team said would be ready after your Dec=
ember release. We've
pencilled in January to try the two together.

Support after launch. We'd suggest each side keeps its own support line, with=
 a shared page
listing who to call for which fault. A booking that never reaches the kiosk i=
s ours; a guest who
can't sign in is yours. Anything in between we'd look at together on a short =
call.

Testing plan. We'd like one full week with both systems running in your demo =
office: real
bookings, real guests, and someone at reception noting anything odd. Our deve=
loper and our
support lead would join a short call each morning that week. We'll write up t=
he results within
five working days of the test.

So, the main question for you: which week in January suits your team for the =
joint end-to-end test?

